Subject: Size an empty C++ class the way the target sizes it

A class whose fields and bases are all empty reaches codegen as `zero_sized`,
and bindgen writes it out as one `_address` byte: the address every distinct
object needs, and the whole of what the class was assumed to need. The target
does not always agree. Two subobjects of the same type need distinct addresses
of their own, so a class deriving from two classes which each derive from the
same empty class holds two copies of it - `struct E {}; struct L : E {}; struct
R : E {}; struct D : L, R {};` makes `sizeof(D)` two under the Itanium ABI, and
three where a third such base is added. An empty class can also be
over-aligned: `struct alignas(8) E {};` is eight bytes wide, and so is anything
deriving from it.

bindgen's own layout assertions are written from the size clang reports, so for
either shape the bindings it writes do not compile. With `--no-layout-tests`
they do, and a Rust caller then holds an object smaller than the one C++ hands
it - the bytes past the first belong to the next object as far as Rust is
concerned.

The size and alignment are already in hand; they are what those assertions are
written from. Pad the class out to that size and give it that alignment, the
two steps every other struct already gets, rather than stopping at the address
byte.

`pad_struct` had to become exact for that to hold. It asks `blob` for
`padding_bytes` bytes at the struct's own alignment, and `blob` hands back
whole units of the alignment it is asked for: seven bytes at alignment four is
one `u32`, three bytes short, and Rust's own alignment padding happens to make
up exactly the difference in every case which could reach it before. Seven
bytes at alignment eight is a wrapper which rounds its own size back up to
eight, which is one byte too many and which nothing makes up. Ask for byte
units where the run is not a whole number of alignment units, and the field is
the run; the struct's alignment is `requires_explicit_align`'s business either
way.

--- a/codegen/mod.rs
+++ b/codegen/mod.rs
@@ -2583,15 +2583,33 @@
             };
 
             if has_address {
-                let layout = Layout::new(1, 1);
+                let address_layout = Layout::new(1, 1);
                 struct_layout.saw_field_with_layout(
                     "_address",
-                    layout,
+                    address_layout,
                     /* offset = */ Some(0),
                 );
                 fields.push(quote! {
                     pub _address: u8,
                 });
+
+                // One byte is the usual answer, not the only one. Two
+                // subobjects of the same type need distinct addresses, so a
+                // class deriving from two classes which each derive from the
+                // same empty class holds two of it and is two bytes wide,
+                // and an empty class can be over-aligned besides. Take the
+                // size and alignment the target gives the class rather than
+                // assuming the address byte accounts for both.
+                if let Some(layout) = layout {
+                    if let Some(padding_field) =
+                        struct_layout.pad_struct(layout)
+                    {
+                        fields.push(padding_field);
+                    }
+                    if struct_layout.requires_explicit_align(layout) {
+                        explicit_align = Some(layout.align);
+                    }
+                }
             }
         }
 
--- a/codegen/struct_layout.rs
+++ b/codegen/struct_layout.rs
@@ -330,8 +330,18 @@
             {
                 // We've already given up on alignment here.
                 Layout::for_size(self.ctx, padding_bytes)
-            } else {
+            } else if padding_bytes % layout.align == 0 {
                 Layout::new(padding_bytes, layout.align)
+            } else {
+                // `blob` hands back whole units of the alignment it is asked
+                // for, so a run of bytes which is not a whole number of them
+                // does not come back as itself: up to 4-byte alignment the
+                // type is short by the remainder, and at 8 the wrapper rounds
+                // its own size up and the type is longer than the run instead.
+                // Byte units are the run exactly, and the struct's alignment
+                // is `requires_explicit_align`'s business rather than this
+                // field's.
+                Layout::new(padding_bytes, 1)
             };
 
             debug!("pad bytes to struct {}, {layout:?}", self.name);
